From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:29:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] gallium: reclaim a driver's memory and retry a refused
 request

A driver refuses an allocation when the process's budget is full, and a
budget that is full often still holds memory for work that has finished:
buffers a finished batch references, storage the driver caches for
reuse. A request dropped for it is lost without a word.

u_reclaim is how a caller that can run its request again does so.
u_reclaim_begin() flushes the caller's context, waits for the work it
submitted, 2 s in all, trims the context, so that completed work
releases what it holds, and releases what the screen caches. Work that
does not finish in time ends the reclaim and rules out another for ten
seconds, since a GPU that is not finishing is not worth waiting for
again soon. The caller retries, and while that fails and
u_reclaim_settle() allows it, retries again a few milliseconds later:
the room a release frees can reach the host's budget a quarter of a
second after the release. The wait is over after 300 ms, or after 20 ms
when the reclaim freed nothing worth waiting for, which a screen
reports through the new optional pipe_screen::get_freed_memory. A
reclaim that did not help rules out another for 250 ms, since a budget
that is really full gains nothing from it and a caller that keeps
meeting the refusal would otherwise finish its context for every
request.

u_texture_subdata_try() is u_default_texture_subdata() that says whether
it wrote.

The gallium-reclaim test runs u_reclaim against a context and a screen
that answer as it says: the steps and the bound of a reclaim, the grace
and the end of the retries, the damping, and a fence that never signals.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
